From b4ce3c2e8f5601549e7bb311b34490a03cdd8913 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 23:22:08 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 184/209] feat(area): sphere shape component

CSphereShapeComponent is the component form of EditorQt's CAreaSphere, minus
the area semantics (id, group, priority, fade, links belong to the Area
function component, decision 03): Radius metres around Offset in the
component's local space, Singleton, category "Area", label "Shape: Sphere".

Every contract answer is analytic - no point list and no sampling, which is
what makes a sphere worth its own kind rather than a many-sided polygon:
containment, the signed hull distance the fade distances will be built on, ray
intersection and a uniformly distributed interior point (direction on the unit
sphere times r * cbrt(u), so the points do not crowd the centre) are all closed
form. GetWorldAABB is exact rather than a transformed-AABB approximation.

GetContour is documented as a convention, not an accident: a 32-segment circle
of the shape's radius on the LOCAL XY plane through the centre - the sphere's
equator in its own space. It is the outline a user would trace on the ground,
it matches what Box returns (its bottom quad), and it is what the point tool
draws as the hull.

EditorHitTest picks the SURFACE and not the volume, a band of `tolerance`
around the radius, which is CAreaSphere::HitTest (AreaSphere.cpp:86-104). A
solid test would make a big sphere swallow every click inside it - the same
reasoning the polygon's hit test follows.

IShapeComponentEdit is a fixed two-point set, so the generic point tool resizes
a sphere without knowing it is one: point 0 is the centre (dragging it moves
the sphere inside its entity), point 1 a radius handle on local +X. The
previewer draws the three great circles of SDisplayContext::DrawWireSphere.

Scale policy (new, recorded in ShapeDisplay.h and in report 03). Entity scale
is honoured by every kind whose maths can represent it exactly, and only by
those:

- Box honours non-uniform scale - a box under an affine transform is still a
  box - as UE's UBoxComponent does;
- Polygon bakes it into the points through the same matrix;
- Sphere cannot: an ellipsoid is not a sphere, and every analytic answer above
  would have to become an ellipsoid solver to stay correct. It takes the
  LARGEST axis scale instead and warns once per component instance, which is
  O3DE's documented behaviour (research/05 section 1.6). The warning is latched
  because a user dragging a scale gizmo would otherwise fill the console at
  frame rate; ENTITY_EVENT_XFORM releases the latch so a new non-uniform scale
  is reported again.

Also in this commit, the display-colour parity of stage 1c item 4a: Box and
Polygon stop drawing in an invented green. ShapeDisplay.h reproduces
CShapeObject's colours for an engine module, which only gets
SEntityPreviewContext::bSelected (EntityObject.cpp:2839 sets it from
CBaseObject::IsSelected()) and an aux renderer: ColorB(0, 204, 255) at alpha
0.8 when unselected (ShapeObject.cpp:922 and :1311), and the pulsing magenta of
SDisplayContext::GetSelectedColor (DisplayContext.cpp:629) when selected.
Reproducing the pulse rather than picking a static pink is what makes a
component shape look like a legacy shape standing next to it.

